WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.337 --> 00:00:02.210
(dramatic music)

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:03.727
- [Narrator] Much like today's birds,

00:00:03.727 --> 00:00:07.377
pterosaurs ruled Earth's Mesozoic skies.

00:00:07.377 --> 00:00:09.000
(pterosaur cawing)

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.610
Adapting to many different habitats

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:14.053
while their dinosaur cousins roamed below.

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:16.623
But these were no birds.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.840
Pterosaurs were flying reptiles,

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.213
and some were as big as fighter jets.

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:27.770
Pterosaurs means winged lizard in Greek,

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:30.100
and they were the very
first vertebrates on Earth

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.410
to take to the skies.

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:35.750
The most well-known pterosaur
is the pterodactycl.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:39.040
It was the first one discovered
back in the 18th century,

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.980
but since then,
paleontologists have uncovered

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:46.080
more than 200 different
species, including pteranodons,

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.180
which were quite large,
and quetzalcoatlus,

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:51.433
one of the most massive pterosaurs of all.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:55.500
Named for the Aztec winged serpent god,

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:58.740
quetzalcoatlus had a
wingspan of nearly 40',

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.510
and likely soared through the skies,

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:03.290
hunting for baby dinosaurs below.

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:07.150
Some, like anurognathus,
were the size of small birds,

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:09.743
and probably preferred to eat insects.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.320
With such a wide range in pterosaur size,

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.830
there is ongoing debate
over how some species

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:20.150
were able to fly, but similarities
in their wing structure

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:22.323
and anatomy may offer clues.

00:01:23.574 --> 00:01:24.990
Pterosaur wings ran along their sides

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:28.470
from shoulder to ankle,
and each wing was held up

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:31.690
and supported by an elongated digit.

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:33.530
The first pterosaur ever discovered

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:36.520
was actually named for
this characteristic.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:38.714
Pterodactyl is a combination
of the Greek words

00:01:38.714 --> 00:01:41.680
for wing and finger.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:44.402
Each pterosaur wing
consisted of a tough membrane

00:01:44.402 --> 00:01:48.277
laced with blood vessels,
fibrous cords, and muscles,

00:01:48.277 --> 00:01:50.620
and it has been theorized that the muscles

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:53.440
could alter the shape of
their wings in mid-flight,

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.340
the same way a passenger
jet might adjust its wings

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.123
during takeoff and landing.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:04.450
Pterosaur fossils also suggest
that even the largest species

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:07.180
must've been relatively
light for their size,

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:10.883
because, much like birds,
their bones were hollow.

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:15.823
Hollow bones would've enabled
even quetzalcoatlus to soar.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.580
But, not all pterosaurs could fly.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:24.590
In 2009, paleontologists
found an enormous specimen

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:27.623
in Transylvania that
they nicknamed Dracula.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:32.990
At 11.5' tall, with a wingspan of 39',

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:36.200
it's one of the largest
pterosaurs ever found.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.140
But, the shape of its shoulders and wings

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:40.793
suggest it probably
couldn't get off the ground.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.480
With every new discovery,
paleontologists are learning more

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.463
about the nature of pterosaurs.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:52.580
But, the hollow bones
that enabled some of the

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:56.010
largest pterosaurs to fly
are also part of the reason

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.473
their fossils are so often incomplete.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:03.050
And, the full picture of a
sky once ruled by reptiles

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:04.763
has yet to be revealed.

